\details{
\subsection{The terra-draw provider}{

With \code{provider = "terra-draw"}, mapgl renders its own toolbar (Terra Draw is
a headless library) with one button per requested mode, a trash button, and
an optional download button. \code{\link[=add_terradraw_control]{add_terradraw_control()}} is an equivalent
convenience wrapper whose signature contains only the arguments that apply
to this provider. Behavioral notes:
\itemize{
\item The trash button deletes the currently selected feature and does nothing
when no feature is selected; use \code{\link[=clear_drawn_features]{clear_drawn_features()}} to remove
everything.
\item When the mode set includes \code{"select"}, finishing a shape returns to the
select tool with the new feature selected (set
\code{terradraw_options(keep_mode_active = TRUE)} to keep drawing instead).
\item Colors are coerced to 6-digit hex (Terra Draw requires hex), so R color
names work but alpha channels are ignored; use \code{fill_opacity} for
transparency.
\item Features loaded via \code{source} or \code{\link[=add_features_to_draw]{add_features_to_draw()}} are adapted to
Terra Draw's constraints: Multi* geometries are split into single-part
features, coordinates are rounded to 9 decimal places (Terra Draw's
precision limit, about 0.1 mm), and polygon interior rings (holes) are
removed.
\item Changing the map style preserves drawn features, but discards an
unfinished drawing and clears the current selection and undo history.
\item The \code{"curve"} and \code{"curve-linestring"} modes draw shapes that mix
straight and curved (cubic Bezier) edges, pen-tool style: click places a
corner point; click-and-drag places an anchor and pulls out symmetric
curve handles (drag distance sets the curvature); moving the mouse
previews the pending segment; click the first point (the last point for
lines) or press Enter to finish; Escape cancels; Backspace removes the
last point. The stored feature uses the rendered curved coordinates, so
measurements, the download button, and \code{\link[=get_drawn_features]{get_drawn_features()}} work
unchanged, and the curve's control points are preserved in a
\code{curveNodes} JSON-string property. In select mode curve features can be
moved as a whole (their control points move with them) but vertex
editing, rotate, and scale are disabled for them, and self-crossing
curve outlines cannot be finished as polygons. While a curve tool is
active, left-drag places curved anchors, so map panning is suspended
until you switch tools.
\item \code{bezier}, \code{bezier_polygon}, and \code{simplify_freehand} are specific to
mapbox-gl-draw and error under terra-draw. \code{attributes} and
\code{show_measurements} work with both providers on standalone widgets (as
with the default provider, neither is available in compare views).
}
}

\subsection{Bezier modes (mapbox-gl-draw provider only)}{

Bezier drawing modes are supported when the draw control is added to the
original map widget or later through a regular Shiny map proxy. Compare
widgets and compare proxies are not yet supported for Bezier modes.

To draw Bezier curves, click the Bezier button, then use \strong{Alt + left-drag}
to create nodes with handles. A plain left-click creates nodes without
handles. Press Enter, or click the last node, to finish the curve. In direct
select mode, select a node and drag its handles to edit the curve; use
\strong{Alt + drag} on a handle to break handle symmetry.

Retrieved Bezier features are returned to R as standard sf geometries using
the rendered curved coordinates: Bezier curves become LineString features and
Bezier polygons become Polygon features. The Bezier control metadata is also
preserved in feature-property columns so the browser widget can continue to
edit those features as Bezier objects.

When \code{attributes} is supplied, selecting exactly one drawn feature opens a
small attribute editor. Click Save to write values to the feature properties;
\code{get_drawn_features()} returns those properties as sf columns. The editor
works for newly drawn features and features loaded into the draw control with
\code{source} or \code{add_features_to_draw()}. Compare widgets are not yet supported
for attribute editing.
}
}
